\b\fs24 Local NLP\b0\fs20\par
Dictionaries/constants: NLP_STOP_WORDS, POSITIVE_WORDS, NEGATIVE_WORDS, NEGATION_WORDS, INTENSIFIERS, INTENT_PHRASES, INTENT_KEYWORDS, QUESTION_STARTERS.\b0\fs20\par
\b0\fs20\par
analyze_text(text):\b0\fs20\par
    1. Applies Unicode NFKC normalization, casefolding and trimming.\b0\fs20\par
    2. Tokenizes with a Unicode-aware regex, preserving internal apostrophes; curly apostrophes are normalized.\b0\fs20\par
    3. Removes stop words, selected contractions and single-character tokens for meaningful-token analysis.\b0\fs20\par
    4. Counts tokens and creates adjacent two-word phrase candidates.\b0\fs20\par
    5. Ranks phrases/tokens by occurrence/position and returns up to eight unique keywords/phrases.\b0\fs20\par
    6. Scores intent from manually defined phrase and keyword dictionaries. A troubleshooting phrase has priority; otherwise the first matching phrase intent wins, then the highest keyword score. With no matching rule it returns generic question or general.\b0\fs20\par
    7. Computes basic lexicon sentiment. A positive/negative word preceded by a negation within three tokens is polarity-flipped; an intensifier within two tokens doubles weight.\b0\fs20\par
    8. Labels question type from the first word (who/what/when/where/why/how/which/whose/whom) or punctuation.\b0\fs20\par
    9. Returns fields nlp_tokens, nlp_keywords, nlp_intent, nlp_question_type, nlp_sentiment.\b0\fs20\par
\b0\fs20\par
Limitations: this is hand-authored rule/keyword analysis, not a learned classifier. Dictionary order and overlapping categories affect labels. Sentiment heuristics can misread negation scope, sarcasm and complex sentences. Intent is metadata/logging only; it does not choose the actual answer. Repeated phrase_candidates.count() can be quadratic for long messages, though chat messages are usually short.\b0\fs20\par
